{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789254278,"nonce":"/WKXriwetfuMK5ooPY0kjUYgpe0=","title":"Standard libraries ship as HIR, lowered and optimised once at build time","message":"The snapshot (zyntax_embed::Snapshot, ZSNAP v3) carries a language's\ngrammar and its modules as parsed TypedPrograms. That skips the parser,\nand nothing else: every compile merges the imported declarations into\nthe program and lowers, optimises and generates code for the library\nagain. For a Python program of two lines the library is 353\ndeclarations; lowering, the HIR pipeline and Cranelift each take tens\nof milliseconds on it, and the frontend's own work is a few. The same\nholds for ZynML's prelude, tensor and simd modules. The library is the\ncompile.\n\nRayzor solved this shape: a separate workspace binary (snapshot-gen)\nlowers the standard library against a warm-up program once per tier\nand writes an archive of per-module IR artifacts (postcard, .blade)\nkeyed by \u003cconfig discriminator\u003e/\u003cmodule\u003e; the compiler's build script\nstages that archive into OUT_DIR and include_bytes! embeds it, an\nempty archive when none exists, so a fresh checkout still lowers from\nsource; every entry carries the build id of the compiler that produced\nit and a consumer rejects one from any other build; cross-module maps\n(functions, fields, vtables, inline constants) are keyed by name so\nids can be re-resolved in the consuming compilation.\n\nDesign for Zyntax:\n\n1. Snapshot v4 adds a per-module HIR section: `serialize_module`\n   (bytecode.rs, postcard) of the module lowered on its own, with\n   `run_interp_safe_opts` and `run_native_only_opts` already applied,\n   so inlining into user code copies optimised bodies. Generic\n   functions stay as templates; `monomorphize_module` instantiates\n   them from user call sites as it does today. The TypedProgram stays\n   in the snapshot with function bodies stripped: the import chain,\n   trait and impl registration and the frontends' typing need the\n   signatures and struct layouts, not the bodies.\n2. Install: `deserialize_module` already advances the HirId counter;\n   `Snapshot::reserve_type_ids` already covers TypeIds. Lowering seeds\n   `SymbolTable.functions` (and globals, effects, handlers) from the\n   library module and appends its functions to the program module\n   instead of lowering the declarations that arrived pre-lowered.\n   Cross-module calls are `HirCallable::Function(HirId)`, so the ids\n   in the archive are the ids user code calls.\n3. Validity: an entry records a build id derived from the compiler\n   crates' sources (rayzor: FNV over src, parser, stdlib) plus the\n   lowering config that shaped it (builtins map, krio_async, plugin\n   signature fingerprint, target pointer width). Any mismatch falls\n   back to the TypedProgram path silently.\n4. Producer: the build script of a language crate can lower, since\n   zynml's build.rs already links zyntax_embed (rayzor could not\n   because its compiler links its runtime cdylib). Snapshot builder\n   gains `.module_lowered(name, program, hir)`; `library(\u0026Policy)`\n   for Python is lowered by zyntax_python's build script per Policy,\n   and its exception prelude joins it.\n5. Codegen still compiles the whole merged module in the tiered\n   runtime; pair with `set_only_compile_reachable` (see the python\n   perf issue) so the archive's unreached bodies are declarations\n   only.\n\nOpen questions: whether the optimised library HIR inlines correctly\nacross the seam (the inliner reads bodies from the same module, so\nyes once merged); how hot reload versions a function that came from\nthe archive; whether the LLVM tier wants the same archive (HIR is\ntier-neutral, so one archive serves all).","files":null}]}